# Windows build and debugging

Windows x64 is the supported application host. Build in WSL Bash from
`/mnt/d/Source/ROMAW`, the same checkout as `D:/Source/ROMAW`. WSL generators and
isolated Linux test harnesses do not establish support for another application host.

## Prerequisites

Install GNU Make, native GCC/G++, MinGW x86_64 GCC/G++ and binutils, Python 3,
Perl, Bison, Flex, CMake, and Autoconf/Automake/Libtool for configuration
regeneration. Syn68k's FPSP image also requires m68k Linux GCC/binutils
(`m68k-linux-gnu-` by default; override with `M68K_TOOL_PREFIX`). Native generators
must match the target pointer width; the wrappers select it automatically.
Python Pillow with FreeType is needed for artwork and the classic font suitcase;
see `system/requirements-native-finder.txt`.

The native Finder's StuffIt folder-transfer encoder also needs Rust and its
`x86_64-pc-windows-gnu` target (`rustup target add x86_64-pc-windows-gnu`).
The root build compiles the pinned library from `stuffit/third_party/` into the
selected object tree. See `stuffit/README.md` for provenance and bridge checks.
The current Rust Windows target requires Windows 10 or later.

Prepare the local SDL dependency prefix:

```bash
cd /mnt/d/Source/ROMAW
bash tools/build-windows-sdl.sh
```

This helper builds checksum-pinned SDL 1.2.15 for `x86_64-w64-mingw32` in
`win64-prefix`. The application wrappers reject other Windows targets.
`WINDOWS_PREFIX` selects another prefix; `CC`, `PPC_CXX` and `BUILD_CC` select
ROMAW's target C, target C++ and native generator compilers. Syn68k uses
`CC_FOR_BUILD` for its Linux generator. Wrappers reconfigure when compiler,
target or prefix settings change to avoid mixing object formats.

SDL's `sdl-config` and pkg-config metadata resolve discovery paths from their
installed location. Python virtual environments and older libtool metadata can
still contain absolute paths; recreate or rebase those when moving an existing
environment. Check `win64-prefix/bin/sdl-config --prefix` before building.

## Build profiles and output

```bash
mkdir -p build
JOBS=10 make > build/romaw-build.log 2>&1
make debug                  # separate -O0 build with symbols
make -C system configure  # configure the selected ROMAW profile
make clean                  # clean the selected profile and syn68k output
```

The root build first builds/stages syn68k, then ROMAW, its PPC bridge and
helper libraries. The default is serial; `JOBS` enables compilation parallelism
after the wrapper's serial generator phase. Close any running staged executable
before linking/staging, and do not run overlapping builds against shared output.

| Profile | ROMAW objects | PPC objects | Staged executable |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Optimized (default) | `build/romaw-optimized/obj` | `build/romaw-optimized/ppc` | `windows-binaries/bin/ROMAW.exe` |
| Debug | `system/obj` | `synppc/obj` | `windows-binaries/bin/ROMAW-debug.exe` |

The optimized executable also has an identical `ROMAW-optimized.exe` alias.
Optimized ROMAW, PPC bridge, Carbon and CMake helpers use
`-O2 -g -fno-strict-aliasing`, with assertions and normal floating-point rules.
Finder and CPU engines retain their own optimization settings.
`ROMAW_BUILD_MODE=debug` selects debug for ordinary configure, library and
clean operations. Object overrides must remain within normal object roots or
workspace `build/`; source directories and symlink escapes are rejected.

Syn68k always builds in `syn68k/obj` and installs headers/archive in
`win64-prefix`. Its Windows `syngen.exe` and `libsyn68k.a` are also staged beside
ROMAW. `make -C syn68k` builds only that dependency; relink ROMAW afterward.
Standalone PPC commands are in [synppc/README.md](synppc/README.md).
For targeted archive builds and ownership, see [static libraries](docs/static-libraries.md).

Generated tables and asset includes live in the profile's `obj/generated/`.
Edit inputs and generators. After Automake input changes run `autoreconf -fi`
at the root before building; never configure in source directories.

`ROMAW_STAGE_DIR` selects isolated staging. The wrapper copies matching SDL
and compiler thread-runtime DLLs only after linking. Keep those DLLs, staged
`fonts/`, `ROMAW.rsrc`, master preferences/mappings and license files
with the executable. `ROMAW_PROFILE_DIR` supplies a WSL path for per-user
asset staging; without Windows interop and without this override, that staging
is skipped. Existing populated profiles are preserved.

## Run and debug

```powershell
& D:/Source/ROMAW/windows-binaries/bin/ROMAW.exe
& D:/Source/ROMAW/windows-binaries/bin/ROMAW.exe -debug trace "PATH/TO/CLASSIC APP"
& D:/Source/ROMAW/windows-binaries/bin/ROMAW.exe --help
```

No-argument startup initializes the shared Toolbox and opens the native Finder.
The SDL presentation window stays hidden. Apps opened from Finder run in separate
ROMAW processes. An application operand launches that app directly.
Runtime installation files resolve beside the executable even for PATH launches
from another directory; mutable state uses the user profile.

Normal launches are GUI-only and leave diagnostic files untouched. `-debug trace`
(or categories such as `-debug unimp,trace`) opens a console and writes
`stdout.txt`/`stderr.txt` in the launch working directory. Logs can be large.
`--help` and `-help` print options and exit before SDL/configuration startup.

For exceptions, inspect `stderr.txt` for `win_exception` and obtain `bt` in
Windows GDB. Debug symbols remain in both profiles. Use
`set substitute-path /mnt/d/Source/ROMAW D:/Source/ROMAW` to resolve WSL sources.
Run `bash system/tests/check-build.sh debug-info` to check DWARF preservation.

## Validation and cleanup

Use [the regression guide](system/tests/README.md) to select focused checks.
`make check` coordinates build/runtime checks; `make check-all` includes CPU,
PPC and UI suites. These targets build prerequisites once and retain per-step
logs. Application startup does not establish full interactive compatibility.

Compare profiles with `bash system/tests/check-graphics.sh toolbox-benchmark`
after building both. It alternates private-profile runs and checks pixel/text
results; samples and hashes go under `build/toolbox-comparison.*`. Timings exclude
startup, translation warm-up and presentation and are not whole-app speedups.
`bash system/tests/check-build.sh build-profiles` checks profile isolation.

Preview old generated test artifacts in PowerShell with
`./tools/clean-build.ps1 -IncludeTestArtifacts`; add `-Apply` to remove selected
paths. `-MinimumAgeHours` defaults to 24. The tool checks ages, running processes,
tracked files and reparse points, preserving production objects, staged binaries,
toolchains, backups and references. Verify it with `./tools/test-clean-build.ps1`.

Everyday settings and file handling are documented in
[the runtime guide](docs/runtime-guide.md), rather than repeated here.
